   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Use context to identify forms",
     "texto": [
      "Many speakers in Spain use **vosotros** for an informal group; **ustedes** is usual across much of the Americas and also appears in parts of Spain. **Vos** is used in several American regions, with forms that vary. Bus vocabulary also varies: **autobús**, **camión** and **colectivo** are used in different places, but usage can differ by city and context.\n\nThe examples below are fictional and show broad clues, not rules for every speaker or country."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Address and the recent past",
     "texto": [
      "**Address.** The same message changes with the group you speak to.\n\n- **Madrid**: *¿Vosotros habéis visto el aviso?*\n- **Mexico City**: *¿Ustedes ya la probaron?*\n- **Buenos Aires**: *¿Vos ya la probaste?*\n\n**Past of today.** In the Madrid version, **ha abierto** presents the news as close to the present. In the other two the indefinido is used (**abrieron**, **inauguraron**) to tell what happened today. It is a regional preference, not a rule for everyone.\n\n**Vocabulary.** *Línea*, *ruta* and *colectivo* name a transport route; *autobús* is the most general word."
     ],
